\\\ {[-\\frac{5}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-15t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{180(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{5}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-15x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{540(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3})\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425645 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425647 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425662 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425666 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1069 "q-342264" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ 3, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1072 #uuid "3df34aa2-66f8-53fb-b84c-e23ff9464f43" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425676 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/7a/7afa339233c7e21eda233f5b67ccf8be9e05fb4ce9484c98eaf59eb790e651ab.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}x=-2\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}3=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statements I and III are false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/1a/1ac6956ec22cfed2d304cc5c2352cf1fdf4c795ea423e7a19c8e3a3e2dc843ed.png)" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425642 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425653 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425665 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425673 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1069 "q-342245" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x, & x\\in [-2,0) \\\\ {x}^{3}, & x\\in [0,2)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-2,2).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1072 #uuid "46096919-32d4-552d-ad62-e0e1723dd83b" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425677 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-2,2).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/0a/0a29065f008fac7d3fd32d52b2ddc0b7d9360ffd75475f6c169eeb26582d22d2