xt{false}.$ The function $F(x)$ has a jump discontinuity at $x=0.$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/2d/2d30d760181c95dd0764b9959ca91febb34e94618bb147d8022dae5745c38a1a.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-2,2).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{true}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}((x+2)^{3}+3)=(0)^{3}+3=3\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}(x+1)=2+1=3.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{2}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {2}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement I is false.\n\n**Note:** The $4$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/fc/fcdf5654322a6deff9d7598590b4e0e1ace6150020b4944ad844976fb0f70230.png)" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425643 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425654 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425657 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425660 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1069 "q-343397" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $2$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=-7{x}^{2}$ defined on $[-1,1).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $2$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=-\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n-7{x}^{2}∼-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n-\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3}∼-\\frac{7}{3}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1072 #uuid "a8bfe2ba-a961-5858-9456-3382dda97cd5" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425679 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=1$ and we are already given the coefficients for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi x).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-1,1).$ \nTherefore, the $2$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-7{t}^{2})\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\int }_{0}^{x}(-\\frac{7}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(n\\pi t))\\text{d}t \\\\ {[-\\frac{7}{3}{t}^{3}]}_{0}^{x} & ∼[-\\frac{7}{3}t]_{0}^{x}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot [\\frac{1}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi t)]_{0}^{x} \\\\ -\\frac{7}{3}{x}^{3} & ∼-\\frac{7}{3}x+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{28(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{3}{\\pi }^{3}}\\cdot \\operatorname{sin}⁡(n\\pi x)\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425650 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425661 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425668 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425674 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1069 "q-342459" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1071 "$$\nF(x)=\\{\\begin{aligned}2x, & x\\in [-3,0) \\\\ {x}^{2}, & x\\in [0,3)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe above function $F(x)$ is defined on $x\\in [-3,3).$ Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R.$\nSelect the correct options below.\n\n| I. | $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-1}} |\n| --- | --- | --- |\n| II. | $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3)$ | {{field-2}} |\n| III. | ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)$ | {{field-3}} |\n| IV. | $f(x)$ is piecewise smooth and continuous on $R$ | {{field-4}} |" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1072 #uuid "c4b9a1ad-55e6-52d3-8f59-ef1cc199427a" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425680 1074 "Let's examine the given statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is continuous on $x\\in (-3,3).$ Its graph is shown below.\n\n![](images/11/11f9718c9384ad2fea0bf3f5cc7da720559c512a5b3cebbc11ca966d5ec0f28b.png)\n\n- Statement II is $\\text{true}.$ The function $F(x)$ is piecewise smooth on $x\\in (-3,3).$ It consists of two smooth pieces.\n\n- Statement III is $\\text{false}.$ Indeed, we have\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}2x=2(-3)=-6\n$$\n\nand \n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x)={lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}{x}^{2}=(3)^{2}=9.\n$$\n\nSo, ${lim}_{x\\to -{3}^{+}}F(x)\\ne {lim}_{x\\to {3}^{-}}F(x).$\n\n- Statement IV is $\\text{false}$ since statement III is false.\n\n**Note:** The $6$-periodic extension of $F(x)$ to $x\\in R$ has the graph shown below.\n\n![](images/d0/d0212ece76aba288e88b0c4d9f2e0f6163f4fd1f594cee30e9ea5f764ebc5f69.png)" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425651 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425667 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425669 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425671 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1069 "q-343337" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $6$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=6x$ defined on $[-3,3).$ Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of a series converging to the $6$-periodic extension of \n\n$$\nG(x)=3{x}^{2}={\\int }_{0}^{x}f(t)\\,\\text{d}t\n$$\n\nfrom the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n6x∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is \n{{field-2}} \non $R,$ we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\n3{x}^{2}∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-3}}\\cdot {{field-4}}.\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1072 #uuid "e9666624-6b3b-5d5b-bd5e-3f9ae5e53130" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425681 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an odd function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{b}_{n}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=3$ and we are already given the coefficients ${b}_{n}$ for $n\\ge 1.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the function $F$ is continuous on $(-3,3).$ \nTherefore, the $6$-periodic extension $f$ is $\\text{piecewise continuous}$ on $R,$ so we may integrate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{x}6t\\,\\text{d}t & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{\\int }_{0}^{x}\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ [3{t}^{2}]_{0}^{x} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{36(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot [-\\frac{3}{n\\pi }\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi t}{3})]_{0}^{x} \\\\ 3{x}^{2} & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\,\\frac{108(-1)^{n+1}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}\\cdot (1-\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\frac{n\\pi x}{3}))\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425644 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425652 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425656 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425659 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1068 #edb/ref 17592186425670 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1069 "q-343062" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1071 "Let $f(x)$ be the $8$-periodic extension of the function $F(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4$ defined on $[-4,4).$  Select the correct options in the reasoning below, showing the derivation of the Fourier series of the $8$-periodic extension of $G(x)=x$ from the corresponding Fourier series of $f(x).$\n\nThe Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4∼\\frac{20}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}{{field-1}}.\n$$\n\nSince $f$ is  \n{{field-2}}\nand  \n{{field-3}}\non $R,$ we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term to get\n\n$$\nx∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{{field-4}}\\cdot {{field-5}}.\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1072 #uuid "fa5cac21-63bb-59e7-aa34-0b7a399b8755" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true] [17592186425682 1074 "First, notice that $F(x)$ is an even function. So, the Fourier series of its $2L$-periodic extension has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{{a}_{0}}{2}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }{a}_{n}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{L}).\n$$\n\nIn our case, $L=4.$ Thus, the Fourier series of $f(x)$ is given by\n\n$$\nf(x)∼\\frac{20}{3}+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\n$$\n\nNow, notice that  \n\n- the function $F$ is continuously differentiable on $(-4,4),$\n\n- continuous on $[-4,4),$ and\n\n- satisfies $F(-4)=F(4),$ if we extend the value at $x=4$ using the formula $F(x)=\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4.$\n\nTherefore, the $8$-periodic extension $f$ is\n$\\text{continuous}$ and\n$\\text{piecewise smooth}$\non $R,$ so we may differentiate its Fourier series term-by-term:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{1}{2}{x}^{2}+4) & ∼\\frac{d}{dx}\\,(\\frac{20}{3})+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{d}{dx}(\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}cos\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})) \\\\ x & ∼0+{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{32(-1)^{n}}{{n}^{2}{\\pi }^{2}}(-\\frac{n\\pi }{4}sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4})) \\\\ x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{-8(-1)^{n}}{n\\pi }sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}) \\\\ x & ∼{\\sum }_{n=1}^{\\infty }\\frac{8(-1)^{n+1}}{n\\pi }\\cdot sin\\,(\\frac{n\\pi x}{4}).\\end{aligned}\n$$" 13194139534557 17592186045417 true]] :edb/tempids {"answer-057eb7f3-190a-5da9-a784-33d6a0e88a3a" 17592186425547 "answer-069197c9-45d7-5029-a771-c125abb39812" 17592186425548 "answer-097c8db1-3ed4-5c62-8384-75b3665d2464" 17592186425549 "answer-0b6c661f-588e-50fc-bfe9-0af2d5d3381b" 17592186425550 "answer-0bdf9085-5e57-5fef-9288-dd2d5b77cfd5" 17592186425551 "answer-0cf9be56-efa9-5355-8976-ae2a23fa9c57" 17592186425552 "answer-1124e7b9-2d5f-5e41-894c-4012a241cbb4" 17592186425553 "answer-113f0528-6296-5edc-a471-87b1ca2ca4b8" 17592186425554 "answer-12ea18be-0a52-5970-8901-dc87b2946b05" 17592186425555 "answer-134b90c6-da6f-564c-b88c-9cde9a9b37af" 17592186425556 "answer-151f3b2c-1198-5804-bb25-a1fa989b21a3" 17592186425557 "answer-15b4446a-3837-52de-923e-6861cab63dfa" 17592186425558 "answer-18a2af04-639a-5b68-ad5f-7ec45b756d4e" 17592186425559 "answer-19361797-c18f-5c79-a0bb-1599c95a5d3a" 17592186425560 "answer-1e34f35d-c327-5601-b6ee-eb5051e0fda7" 17592186425561 "answer-1ef8e8aa-a251-53f0-963a-b684cf7c36a8" 17592186425562 "answer-2145c4be-ed47-597a-9e67-aec8406bd115" 17592186425563 "answer-234f71f2-30af-5f96-9d21-0a216e6cd3a5" 17592186425564 "answer-2560f1d4-c